  "notes": "Memphis pointed at a ringtone, and every override here is that target. The research says the harmony is **brighter and more consonant than the Hypnotize board because the target was radio** — a i–VI–VII vamp with a major-mode hook lift — so this is the only classic-era Memphis entry in the batch whose lead family actually moves through three chords, with sevenths at 16% and a high `chordToneBias` over a low `halfStepDissonance`. The kit is the snap/crunk hybrid: **finger-snap or clap on 3, 16th hats, handclap layers, and a sparse kit with large gaps for the hook**, so `percs.lanes` leads with `snap`, the hats are non-continuous, and the kick stays at two or three. The one purely mix-driven parameter is the 808: the research says **short decay, because ringtone-era mixes need mid definition**, which is why this is the only entry in the batch with `bass808.sustain` set to `staccato` — root-and-fifth rather than a pedal. \"The hook is the product\" is authored as a bright 2-bar pluck ostinato high in the register with a short-phrase bias. ⚠ `low`: the research rates it medium-low and says **every beat parameter is a ringtone-era / crunk-lane inference** — `session.bpm` (72–84 half-time = 144–168 straight, marked [LOW-CONF]), `drums.kick` and the hat base are all inferred. The chart placement and the songwriting credit are what is sourced. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
